#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078220 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "d17b0e14-aabc-5814-a242-d5fb803cb406" :question/math-academy-id "e-263" :question/problem "Given that the function\n${\\sqrt{2b - 3x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ -x + 2,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$\nis continuous at $x=0$, find the value of the constant $b$." :question/worked-solution "At $x=0$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{-})\\sqrt{2b - 3x} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2b - 3(0)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{+})(-x + 2) \\\\\n&= - (0) + 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 0^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x) \\\\\n2 &= \\sqrt{2b} \\\\\n4 &= 2b \\\\\nb &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078222 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "266c7322-6395-5e9b-ad8e-fe51f3c36665" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Polynomial Function"}] :question/id #uuid "90e5fce3-26db-513d-9270-1e60b8dfb0d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-264" :question/problem "Find the value(s) of the constant $a$ that make the function $f(x)$ below continuous at $x=2$.\n${(2x + a)^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 2 \\\\ x^{2} - 3,\\end{vmatrix}x > 2$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=2$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{-})(2x + a)^{2} \\\\\n&= (2(2) + a)^{2} \\\\\n&= (4 + a)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})(x^{2} - 3) \\\\\n&= (2)^{2} - 3 \\\\\n&= 4 - 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=2$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 2^{-})f(x) &= lim_(x → 2^{+})f(x) \\\\\n(4 + a)^{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{(a + 4)^{2}} &= \\sqrt{1} \\\\\n\\mid a + 4 \\mid &= 1 \\\\\na + 4 &= ± 1 \\\\\na &= -4 ± 1 \\\\\na &= -5,-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$ for $a=-5$ or $a=-3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078225 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "237b4039-b516-54cb-ab4d-546578a0b1c2" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Logarithmic Function"}] :question/id #uuid "b19955e9-3e5d-597e-81bc-f8f675e78e9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-265" :question/problem "Given that the function\n${\\log_{2} (ax + 1),\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1 \\\\ \\log_{3} (2x + 1),\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\nis continuous at $x=1$, find the value of $a$." :question/worked-solution "At $x=1$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{-})\\log_{2} (ax + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (a(1) + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{2} (a + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})(\\log_{3} (2x + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (2(1) + 1) \\\\\n&= \\log_{3} (3) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=1$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1^{-})g(x) &= lim_(x → 1^{+})g(x) \\\\\n\\log_{2} (a + 1) &= 1 \\\\\na + 1 &= 2^{1} \\\\\na &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078334 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e155cb07-8d89-5d4b-9015-f73300bab2e9" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Exponential Function"}] :question/id #uuid "aef91e7a-138c-58bc-a25c-ca77284581c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6279" :question/problem "Find the value of the constant $c$ that makes the function $h(x)$ below continuous at $x=4$.\n${4^{cx + 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 4 \\\\ 4x,\\end{vmatrix}x > 4$" :question/worked-solution "At $x=4$, the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4^{-})h(x) &= h(4) \\\\\n&= 4^{c(4) + 1} \\\\\n&= 4^{4c + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 4^{+})h(x) &= lim_(x → 4^{+})(4x) \\\\\n&= 4(4) \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $h(x)$ to be continuous at $x=4$, we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4^{4c + 1} &= 16 \\\\\n4^{4c + 1} &= 4^{2} \\\\\n4c + 1 &= 2 \\\\\n4c &= 1 \\\\\nc &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=4$ for\n\n$$\nc = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55607d46-d93c-5306-9b2e-b9a8e79366eb" :knowledge-point/title "Removing a Jump Discontinuity in a Piecewise Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1590556-df58-5dfa-8cd2-a915d135a923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076851} {:answer/id #uuid "e4a16ad4-103b-57c4-97c3-fbe5192b77ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076852} {:answer/id #uuid "0611abe8-a8e4-56b6-87c4-9c42a04bffb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076853} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfdc5f4-7dd2-509f-b5bb-c17b7138326a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076854} {:answer/id #uuid "56267d71-84a7-5285-a7ad-16ada5b63ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076855}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cf456630-a37f-5980-9139-67494978b5c1" :answer-field/key 